(King Dasharatha looked upon his royal preceptor Sage Vashistha, his voice trembling with an ache that sixty years of) Tj
T* (sovereignty could not soothe. "Vashistha, holy master of our race," he whispered, gazing out through the marble arches) Tj
T* (toward the sacred Sarayu. "Look upon this vast assembly of ministers, vassals, and heroic commanders. Look upon the) Tj
T* (boundless treasury of Kosala, where gold lies heaped like grain. Sixty thousand years of unbroken virtue have I offered to the) Tj
T* (gods. I have performed the Ashwamedha, protected the hermits, and kept the rod of justice stainless. Yet when I look into the) Tj
T* (future of our lineage, I see only cold ash. No laughter of a child echoes through these marble corridors. No tiny hands reach) Tj
T* (up to grasp my mantle when I return from council. Tell me, preceptor, what is a kingdom if it descends into darkness? Who) Tj
T* (will pour the sacred libations to our forefathers when my breath ceases? If my house dies with me, then my entire life has) Tj
T* (been a desert dressed in silk.") Tj
